course: dictionary
lesson: 7556
title: Prejudice: judging before you know
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prejudice is a word you will meet in stories and in the news. Read the context: «Judging a whole group, like the kids from the flats, from one picture is how prejudice starts.» The sentence tells you that prejudice is judging people from one picture, before you know them.
explain: Look at the word itself. The start, pre-, means before, and judge means to form an opinion. So prejudice is an opinion formed before you know the facts. Swap this meaning into the sentence above and it still makes sense.
explain: Read the clues in this sentence: «Jokes that mock people's race, religion or disability cause real hurt and spread prejudice.» Words like hurt and spread tell you that prejudice is unfair and harmful, not just a harmless opinion.
fact: Prejudice means an unfair opinion about a person or group, formed before you know them.
fact: Prejudice is often based on skin colour, religion, where someone comes from, or how they look.
fact: To prejudice someone means to make them have an unfair opinion before they know the facts.
fact: Being «prejudiced» means judging people before you know them.
fact: Prejudice can lead to bullying and unkind treatment, so people learn to recognise it and stop it.
exercise: «Judging a whole group, like the kids from the flats, from one picture is how prejudice starts.» What starts when you judge a whole group from one picture? | A new hobby | *Prejudice | Friendship | A holiday
exercise: «Jokes that mock people's race, religion or disability cause real hurt and spread prejudice.» What does this sentence tell you about prejudice? | *It spreads and causes real hurt | It makes people happy | It is always funny | It is a kind of sport
exercise: Nadia says prejudice means being very clever. What is the real meaning? | A kind thing you do for a friend | Being very clever at maths | *An unfair opinion formed before you know someone
test: Which of these is prejudice? | Sharing your sandwiches at lunch | Helping a friend with their homework | *Deciding a whole group is unkind because of their religion, without ever meeting them
test: A prejudiced person judges someone how? | Only when they win a game | Only on a Monday | After getting to know them well | *Before knowing them
exam: Why is prejudice harmful, according to the lesson? | It helps you make new friends faster | *It can lead to bullying and unkind treatment | It is a sport played in school | It makes people forget their facts
